<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/advanced/cardiovascular-pathophysiology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The foundational lessons gave you the pump and the plumbing: chambers, valves, the conduction
    system, the physics of pressure and flow. Pathophysiology is what happens when those systems are
    perturbed — and the striking discovery of a century of cardiology is that the three great killers
    (atherosclerosis, hypertension, heart failure) are not separate diseases but one connected chain.
    High pressure injures vessel walls; injured walls retain cholesterol; plaques rupture or the
    chronically overloaded muscle fails. Intervene anywhere in the chain and mortality moves.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other theme to carry through: cardiovascular medicine is unusually quantitative. Blood
    pressure is a number, LDL is a number, ejection fraction is a number — and treatment targets are
    set, and audited, in those numbers. Work the arithmetic in every section below; it is the
    discipline's real grammar.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain from risk factor to event — and where interventions bite" widget="equationStep" config={cardioCfg} />

  <h2>Haemodynamics: the equation every therapy obeys</h2>
  <p>
    Mean arterial pressure (MAP) ≈ cardiac output × systemic vascular resistance (SVR). This identity
    organises the entire pharmacology of blood pressure. Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume,
    so: beta-blockers cut rate and contractility; diuretics cut stroke volume by shrinking plasma
    volume; ACE inhibitors and calcium-channel blockers cut resistance by dilating arterioles. Nothing
    else is available — every antihypertensive is a handle on one of those factors, chosen because the
    body pushes the other one back up (the baroreflex resists any univariate assault, which is why
    combination therapy is the norm).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example.</strong> A patient has CO = 5 L/min and MAP = 100 mmHg, so SVR = 20
    mmHg·min per L. Give a vasodilator that drops resistance 25% to 15; the baroreflex raises heart
    rate and stroke volume, lifting CO to 6 L/min — pressure only falls to 90. Block the reflex with a
    beta-blocker at the same time and the fall is real. This reflex-compensation logic explains why
    single-drug therapy often disappoints and why low doses of two or three agents beat escalating
    one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Resistance itself obeys Poiseuille's law: flow scales with the fourth power of radius. Halving an
    arteriole's radius multiplies its resistance sixteenfold — the physical reason a 70% coronary
    stenosis matters so much, and why "minor" narrowings under high flow demand become limiting.
  </p>

  <h2>Atherosclerosis: the twenty-year insult</h2>
  <p>
    Atherosclerosis is an inflammatory lipid disease, and the sequence is now well resolved.
    Endothelium injured (by pressure, smoking toxins, glucose) becomes permeable; LDL particles lodge
    in the intima and are chemically modified; monocytes invade, engulf the modified LDL and become
    foam cells; smooth muscle migrates in and lays down a fibrous cap over a necrotic lipid core. For
    decades the plaque grows <em>outward</em> (vessels remodel to preserve the lumen — Glagov's
    finding), which is why atherosclerosis is silent until it is catastrophic. The event comes in two
    modes: <strong>stable angina</strong> (fixed stenosis limits flow under exertion; pain predictable,
    relieved by rest) and <strong>acute rupture</strong> — the cap erodes, collagen and tissue factor
    are exposed, platelets swarm, and an occlusive thrombus forms within minutes. The chronic plaque
    is the loaded gun; the clot pulls the trigger.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why is LDL the causal agent rather than a passenger? Three independent lines converge: epidemiology
    (log-linear dose-response between LDL and events, with no floor down to genetically low levels);
    Mendelian randomisation (people inheriting lifelong low LDL — via PCSK9 loss-of-function, for
    instance — have ~88% lower coronary risk, far beyond what late statin start achieves); and
    randomised trials (every LDL-lowering modality — statins, ezetimibe, PCSK9 antibodies — reduces
    events in proportion to absolute LDL reduction: roughly 22% fewer major events per 1 mmol/L per
    year of treatment). The operational conclusion: what matters is cumulative exposure, measured in
    "LDL-years" — a person at 4 mmol/L for forty years accumulates the plaque burden of a lifelong
    hypo-cholesterolaemic at 2 mmol/L for eighty. This is why guidelines now talk about starting
    earlier, not just lower.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "hardened arteries" as a plumbing problem solved by roto-rooting.
    Stents relieve obstructive stenoses and save lives during acute infarction, but for stable disease
    the landmark ISCHEMIA trial found that invasive revascularisation did not reduce death or
    infarction versus optimal medical therapy. The disease is in the vessel wall and the blood, not
    the narrowest spot — which is why statins, blood-pressure control and smoking cessation prevent
    more deaths than any catheter lab.
  </p>

  <h2>Hypertension: the silent multiplier</h2>
  <p>
    Hypertension is defined operationally — sustained clinic readings of 140/90 mmHg or above (130/80
    by the 2017 ACC/AHA criteria) — because that is where measured end-organ damage curves begin to
    inflect. It affects well over a billion people; roughly half are unaware. The pathophysiology of
    90–95% of cases (<em>primary</em> hypertension) is distributed: kidney pressure-natriuresis curves
    reset rightward (higher pressures needed to excrete the same salt), amplified by obesity, salt,
    alcohol, age-related vascular stiffening and sympathetic overactivity. The <em>secondary</em> 5%
    has a named culprit — renal artery stenosis, primary aldosteronism, sleep apnoea — and is worth
    hunting because it is curable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The damage is mechanical at every level. Chronically elevated pressure accelerates
    atherosclerosis (the risk-factor chain's first link), thickens the left ventricle
    (concentric hypertrophy), stiffens large arteries (isolated systolic hypertension in the elderly:
    systolic keeps rising while diastolic falls as vessels lose elastic recoil), and finally
    overwhelms small arterioles, producing the retinopathy, nephropathy and lacunar strokes of
    malignant hypertension. Every point of damage scales with the pressure integral over time —
    which is why a 10 mmHg population-wide reduction cuts stroke mortality roughly 40% and why
    asymptomatic people are treated: the treatment's target is an event twenty years away.
  </p>

  <h2>Heart failure: compensation and its cost</h2>
  <p>
    Heart failure is the end state where the pump cannot deliver adequate output at normal filling
    pressures. The classification that governs therapy splits on one number, ejection fraction (EF =
    stroke volume ÷ end-diastolic volume):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HFrEF</strong> (reduced EF, classically under 40%): systolic failure. Infarcted or
    cardiomyopathic muscle contracts weakly. Compensation runs through the Frank–Starling mechanism
    (more stretch, stronger beat) and the neurohormonal axis: falling output activates the sympathetic
    system and the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone cascade, retaining salt and water to raise preload.
    The compensation is the disease's engine — chronic volume overload dilates the ventricle, wall
    stress rises (Laplace: wall stress ∝ pressure × radius), remodelling progresses, and output falls
    further. Modern therapy interrupts precisely this loop: ACE inhibitors/ARNI block angiotensin,
    beta-blockers tame the sympathetic drive, mineralocorticoid antagonists block aldosterone, SGLT2
    inhibitors work through still-being-mapped metabolic paths. Each class added on top of standard
    care delivers a further 20–30% relative mortality reduction — among the largest effects in
    medicine.</li>
    <li><strong>HFpEF</strong> (preserved EF, over 50%): diastolic failure. The ventricle is stiff
    (hypertrophied, fibrotic, often diabetic and obese) and fills poorly, so congestion arrives at
    normal EF. Half of all heart failure; until recently, essentially no evidence-based therapy —
    an honest open problem, with SGLT2 inhibitors now the first class to show credible benefit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example.</strong> A dilated ventricle with end-diastolic volume 250 mL ejecting
    75 mL has EF = 75/250 = 30%. The same stroke volume from a normal 120 mL ventricle is an EF of
    62% — same output, utterly different prognosis and therapy. This is why echocardiography, not
    symptoms, starts every heart-failure pathway: the number chooses the drug classes.
  </p>

  <h2>Acute events: ischaemia, infarction, rhythm</h2>
  <p>
    When a coronary thrombus occludes flow, myocardium dies on a clock — "time is muscle." The
    diagnosis stack is fast: the 12-lead ECG (ST-segment elevation marks transmural infarction;
    a STEMI patient goes straight to the catheter lab), and troponin assays (proteins released by
    dead myocytes, rising within hours, near-perfectly specific for myocardial injury). Reperfusion —
    stenting the culprit artery or thrombolysis — salvages jeopardised muscle at roughly an hour-per-
    mortality-decimal scale: every 30 minutes of delay measurably raises one-year mortality. Then the
    rhythm questions: necrotic substrate generates ventricular fibrillation (the defibrillator's
    non-synchronous reset is the only treatment), and scarred atria sustain atrial fibrillation —
    irregular, ineffective atrial quivering that also stagnates blood in the left atrial appendage,
    creating the stroke risk that anticoagulation (and CHA<sub>2</sub>DS<sub>2</sub>-VASc scoring)
    manages.
  </p>

  <h2>Risk arithmetic in practice: the calculator and the NNT</h2>
  <p>
    Guidelines operationalise prevention through multivariable risk scores (Framingham-derived,
    now pooled-cohort equivalents): age, sex, blood pressure, cholesterol, smoking and diabetes
    combine into a 10-year risk percentage, and the percentage drives decisions — a statin is
    generally recommended at 10-year risk of 10% and above, because absolute benefit scales
    with baseline risk. Work the arithmetic: a 62-year-old smoker with systolic pressure 150
    and total cholesterol 6.5 mmol/L might score 20%; a statin cutting LDL by 1.0 mmol/L
    reduces major vascular events by roughly a quarter, so absolute risk falls about five
    points — number needed to treat ≈ 20 over ten years. The same drug in a 40-year-old at 2%
    risk yields NNT ≈ 200: the clinical decision is the same molecule at a very different
    price. Blood-pressure therapy follows the same structure (each 10 mmHg reduction cuts
    stroke risk roughly 40% at every baseline), and the population version is the most
    consequential public-health arithmetic in medicine: shifting average population blood
    pressure down does more than any single emergency intervention — which is why salt policy,
    tobacco taxation and hypertension case-finding are, institutionally, cardiology.
  </p>

  <h2>From mechanisms to the clinic visit</h2>
  <p>
    The chain above explains what clinicians actually check. The pulse and blood-pressure cuff
    sample the haemodynamic equation; the ECG reads the conduction system and shows
    hypertrophy (voltage criteria) and ischaemia; troponin and echocardiography grade muscle
    and valve function; the lipid panel and HbA1c track the chronic exposures; and the risk
    calculator compresses all of it into the prevention decision. Pathophysiology is not
    background knowledge — it is why the checklist is shaped the way it is, and why a normal
    examination with terrible risk factors still warrants treatment: the medicine targets the
    twenty-year integral, not today's symptoms.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The epidemiological machinery.</strong> Every number above descends from cohort logic —
    above all the Framingham Heart Study (running since 1948), which coined "risk factor" and built
    the multivariable risk functions behind every clinic calculator. Their frailties are the
    intermediate-tier lesson's causal-inference questions: confounding, competing risk, and the
    gap between relative and absolute risk (a 25% relative reduction means everything to a 20%-risk
    patient and little to a 1%-risk one — the number needed to treat makes this explicit).
    <strong>The inflammation frontier.</strong> Lowering LDL further after statins helps, but residual
    risk persists; the CANTOS trial showed an anti-inflammatory antibody (canakinumab) cut events
    without touching cholesterol — opening a second axis, now shared with colchicine prescriptions.
    Whether hsCRP-guided therapy enters guidelines is a live question.
    <strong>HFpEF's enigma</strong> remains the field's most consequential open problem: a
    heterogeneous syndrome (stiffness, microvascular disease, inflammation, obesity phenotypes)
    masquerading as one diagnosis, defeating trial after trial. And the Mendelian-randomisation
    programme is quietly rewriting prevention: lifelong exposure simulations in genetic data keep
    confirming that the age at which risk factors start matters as much as their magnitude — the
    quantitative case for treating prevention as a decades-long project, not a midlife correction.
  </p>

